{
  "teamId": 136,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Seattle Mariners arrived with cautious optimism, carrying a young core headlined by rookie phenom Julio Rodríguez and the promise of a pitching staff that could keep them in games. April was a grind — an 11-10 record that showed both fight and fragility, with early blown saves and clutch singles defining a roster still learning how to win. The month set a template for what would become a season-long tension between a bullpen that could both save and sink them, and an offense that had just enough power in reserve to answer back.\n\nMay brought the first real test, and the Mariners stumbled, going 10-18 in their worst stretch of the year. Paul Sewald, who would become a central figure in the season's drama, gave up crushing home runs, and the offense went quiet at the worst moments. But June flipped the script almost entirely. Eugenio Suárez erupted with power — including his 10th homer in a pivotal June 3rd comeback — and role players like Abraham Toro and Dylan Moore delivered timely hits that salvaged series and steadied the ship. The Mariners went 16-13 in June and found their footing.\n\nJuly and August were when Seattle announced itself as a genuine contender. An 18-7 July, fueled by clutch contributions from Carlos Santana, Cal Raleigh, and more timely hitting from Toro, transformed the Mariners from a hopeful squad into a division-race participant. The 17-10 August further cemented their standing, and when September arrived, Julio Rodríguez and Eugenio Suárez hit back-to-back home runs on September 11th to epitomize the team's resilient identity. The Mariners clinched a playoff berth for the first time since 2001, ending the longest postseason drought in North American professional sports.\n\nThe Wild Card round against Toronto offered a moment of pure joy — J.P. Crawford's clutch double and a series win that sent T-Mobile Park into a frenzy. But the ALDS brought Yordan Alvarez, and with him, heartbreak. Alvarez's three-run bomb off Robbie Ray in Game 1 was a hammer blow, and his second-round homer off Luis Castillo in Game 2 underscored the Mariners' inability to contain Houston's superstar. They fought in Games 3 and 4, but the Astros were simply the better team. Still, what the 2022 Mariners built — 90 wins, a playoff return, and a foundation anchored by Rodríguez and Raleigh — was the beginning of something Seattle fans had waited more than two decades to feel.",
